/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Visual approaches stop when cloud and fog hide the runway.
   - Selecting one localizer frequency tunes the paired glide path automatically.
   - Both transmitters radiate a carrier amplitude-modulated by two tones, 90 Hz and 150 Hz, from two overlapping antenna patterns.
   - DDM is the difference in depth of modulation; positive means the 150 Hz tone is stronger.
   - Localizer sense: seen from an approaching aircraft, 90 Hz predominates to the left of the centreline and 150 Hz to the right.
   - Glide path sense: 90 Hz predominates above the path and 150 Hz below it.
   - Because guidance comes from a difference of two depths on one carrier, it does not depend on how strong the received signal is.
   - A category describes the whole operation (ground equipment, aircraft, crew training), not the receiver alone.
   - Advantages of ILS: one standard worldwide, so every airliner can use every ILS; a cheap, simple airborne receiver; guidance that grows more precise as the aircraft nears the runway; integrity monitoring on the ground; and support for automa
   - Limits: one straight path per runway end; only 40 channels; beams bent by reflections from buildings, terrain and taxiing aircraft, so critical areas must be kept clear in low visibility; and false glide paths at steeper angles above the re
*/
