/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A rotor spinning about its axis carries angular momentum H, a vector along the spin axis.
   - H is angular momentum in kg·m²/s; I is the rotor's moment of inertia about its spin axis in kg·m²; ω is the spin rate in rad/s (rpm × 2π ÷ 60).
   - The rotor sits in gimbals: rings pivoted on bearings so that the case can turn without twisting the rotor.
   - If two gimbal axes line up, one freedom is lost and the gyro cannot follow a turn about the missing axis: gimbal lock.
   - A torque changes angular momentum: T = dH/dt.
   - Ω is the steady precession rate in rad/s; T is the torque component at right angles to the spin axis in N·m; H = Iω.
   - A practical rule gives the direction: picture the force applied at the rim of the rotor, then carry that point 90° round in the direction of spin.
   - Apply a torque suddenly and the axis overshoots, tracing small fast loops around the steady precession path: nutation.
   - Drift is any unwanted wandering of the spin axis.
   - A rate gyro is an SDF gyro whose gimbal is held by a spring of stiffness k.
*/
