/* book Worked example: Q: A directional gyro rotor has I = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ kg·m² and spins at 24 000 rpm. Bearing friction applies a torque of 5 × 10⁻⁶ N·m at right angles to the spin axis. (a) Find H. (b) Find the real drift rate in °/h. (c) At Chennai (13° N) add the apparent drift in the same sense, and find the heading error after 15 minutes.
   1. ω = 24 000 × 2π ÷ 60 = 2513.27 rad/s.
   2. H = Iω = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ × 2513.27 = 0.3016 kg·m²/s.
   3. Ω = T ÷ H = 5 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 0.3016 = 1.658 × 10⁻⁵ rad/s.
   4. In degrees per hour: 1.6579 × 10⁻⁵ × (180 ÷ π) × 3600 = 3.42 °/h of real drift.
   5. Apparent drift at 13° N = 15.04 × sin 13° = 15.04 × 0.2250 = 3.38 °/h.
   6. Total = 3.42 + 3.38 = 6.80 °/h; in 15 minutes the card is off by 6.80 × 0.25 = 1.7°.
   Answer: H = 0.302 kg·m²/s; real drift 3.42 °/h; with 3.38 °/h apparent drift the heading is about 1.7° wrong after 15 minutes, which is why the heading indicator is reset to the compass regularly. */
