/* book Worked example 2: Q: A turn indicator's rate gyro has I = 5 × 10⁻⁵ kg·m² and spins at 12 000 rpm; its spring stiffness is k = 0.04 N·m/rad. (a) Find the gimbal deflection in a rate-one turn (3°/s). (b) What turn rate gives a 10° deflection?
   1. ω = 12 000 × 2π ÷ 60 = 1256.6 rad/s, so H = 5 × 10⁻⁵ × 1256.6 = 0.06283 kg·m²/s.
   2. Rate one: Ω = 3°/s = 0.05236 rad/s. Gyroscopic torque HΩ = 0.06283 × 0.05236 = 3.290 × 10⁻³ N·m.
   3. θ = HΩ ÷ k = 3.290 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.04 = 0.0822 rad = 4.71°.
   4. (b) Ω = kθ ÷ H = 0.04 × 0.1745 ÷ 0.06283 = 0.1111 rad/s = 6.37 °/s.
   Answer: A rate-one turn deflects the gimbal 4.71°; a 10° deflection means 6.37 °/s, a little over rate two. Deflection is proportional to rate, so the scale is linear. */
